@diplomacy
title: DIPLOMACY

Here we can decide to conduct diplomacy with specific countries. 

- #diplomacy
- @foreign_return



@secret_treaties
title: Decide what to do with the secret treaties of the previous governments.  
subtitle: We inherited numerous documents of note...
tags: foreign_special
new-page: true
view-if: secret_treaties != 1

Upon coming to power, we captured a plethora of foreign documents and correspondences dating to both the Imperial Russian government and the Provisional Governemnt of 1917. We now have concrete proof for a number of embarrasing arrangements behind closed doors:

>**Constaninople Agreement** (1915) - Russia was promised (bribed with?) future ownership over Constantinople and the Bosporus straits. And yet the Tsar claimed it was a defense war?!

>**Sykes–Picot Agreement** (1916) - Britain and France have pre-divided the former Ottoman Empire amongst themselves! So much for national self-determination...

>**Treaty of London** (1915) - Italy was allured into war with promises of land from Austria. How callous the imperialists are with human lives!

>And countless other examples of diplomatic correspondence, showing bargaining, demands, and extortion...

Considering we've always decried the dangers of "secret diplomacy" in our propaganda, we now have an interesting opportunity at our disposal. By releasing these documents in full to the world, we can make clear the corrupt and conniving nature of the imperialist powers, increasing anti-war sentiment, and proving our own claims about previous administrations true after all. Of course, said imperialists won't appreciate being exposed. 

- @release_the_treaties
- @bargaining_chips
- @leave_them_be


@release_the_treaties
title: Publish the documents!
on-arrival: secret_treaties = 1; workers_b += 0.5; soldiers_b += 1; leninist_strength += 5; uk_relation -= 25; france_relation -= 25; usa_relation -= 10; italy_relation -= 20; japan_relation -= 5; instability += 1; democraticity += 1; 

The publication of the documents causes an international firestorm. Disdain for our government grows among the international community, but especially in Britain and France, where national leaders are enraged. It is likely this will severely impair our ability to work with these countries in the future.

At the same time, socialists around the world applaud our actions as truly revolutionary—hopefully a sign of growing revolutionary sentiment within their own borders. Domestically, the news surges support within our base and helps to win workers and soldiers over to our side. 

@bargaining_chips
title: Perhaps we can use this info to gain some leverage with the Entente instead. 
subtitle: Begin secret talks with the allies, promising to withhold publication in exchange for favorable terms. 
choose-if: foreign_minister_faction != "Left"
unavailable-subtitle: The current people's commissar is from the radical left-wing of the Party, and won't allow this. 

@leave_them_be
title: Leave them be for now. 
go-to: foreign_affairs


@call_for_peace
title: Issue a statement to all belligerents in the Great War, calling for general peace negotiations. 
tags: foreign_special
new-page: true
max-visits: 1
view-if: call_for_peace != 1 and german_negotiations < 1
on-arrival: {!
  Q.call_for_peace = 1; Q.german_revolution += 1;
  Q.uk_relation -= 4; Q.france_relation -= 4; Q.us_relation -= 2; 
!}

= Call for Peace

The call for peace is issued to all belligerents in the Great War. However, it appears are pleas have fallen on deaf ears, as none of the major powers take the statement seriously. Moreover, the Allied Powers grow uneased by our lack of resolve toward the war effort. 








  
